{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You have a normal HTTP server and you want the chat reply to stream. Use Server-Sent Events (SSE). It is the default transport. It works in all browsers, it passes through most proxies, and it is easy to debug."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"1-return-sse-from-the-server","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1. Return SSE from the server"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wrap the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chat()"},{"type":"text","value":" stream with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toServerSentEventsResponse()"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"2-connect-from-the-client","children":[{"type":"text","value":"2. Connect from the client"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchServerSentEvents()"},{"type":"text","value":" to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n});","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To add headers, a dynamic URL, or extra body data, see "},{"type":"link","href":"./request-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Request Options"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"resume-a-dropped-stream","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resume a dropped stream"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A connection can drop in the middle of a reply. If the server sends SSE "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id:"},{"type":"text","value":" values, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchServerSentEvents"},{"type":"text","value":" can continue the reply:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconnect."}]},{"type":"text","value":" If the connection drops after an id arrived, the adapter reconnects with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Last-Event-ID"},{"type":"text","value":". It removes the replayed chunks that it already has."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun(runId)"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"text","value":" It sends a read-only GET with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"offset=-1"},{"type":"text","value":" and the run id. The server replays an in-flight or finished run from the start."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The ids appear only when the server passes a durability adapter to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"toServerSentEventsResponse"},{"type":"text","value":". The ids are opaque tokens that the durability adapter owns. The chat client does not create, parse, or store them. Without ids, the adapter does one plain fetch."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" handler next to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"POST"},{"type":"text","value":", so that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"joinRun"},{"type":"text","value":" works in a second tab or after a reload:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"POST"}]},{"type":"text","value":" starts new runs. It also gets the automatic reconnects, because the adapter sends the same body again with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Last-Event-ID"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"}]},{"type":"text","value":" replays a known run from the start."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  resumeServerSentEventsResponse,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({ adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"), messages, threadId, runId });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n  });\n}\n\n// joinRun hits GET ?offset=-1&runId=... (replay only, no messages sent).\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  return resumeServerSentEventsResponse({ adapter: memoryStream(request) });\n}","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"GET"},{"type":"text","value":" handler does not call a provider. On a replay, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeFrom()"},{"type":"text","value":" on the durability adapter is not null (it comes from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"?offset"},{"type":"text","value":"). So the handler replays the log. If the request has no resume offset, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resumeServerSentEventsResponse"},{"type":"text","value":" returns a 400."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Other adapters resume the same way:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xhrServerSentEvents"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id:"},{"type":"text","value":" lines. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./react-native","children":[{"type":"text","value":"React Native"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetchHttpStream"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"xhrHttpStream"},{"type":"text","value":" resume over NDJSON. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./http-stream#resume-a-dropped-stream","children":[{"type":"text","value":"HTTP Stream"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To pick a durability adapter for production, see "},{"type":"link","href":"../resumable-streams/overview","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Resumable Streams"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Send a message and turn off the network for a moment. When the network comes back, the reply continues with no duplicate text."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"1-return-sse-from-the-server","text":"1. Return SSE from the server","level":2},{"id":"2-connect-from-the-client","text":"2. Connect from the client","level":2},{"id":"resume-a-dropped-stream","text":"Resume a dropped stream","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nYou have a normal HTTP server and you want the chat reply to stream. Use Server-Sent Events (SSE). It is the default transport. It works in all browsers, it passes through most proxies, and it is easy to debug.\n\n## 1. Return SSE from the server\n\nWrap the `chat()` stream with `toServerSentEventsResponse()`:\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\n## 2. Connect from the client\n\nPass `fetchServerSentEvents()` to `useChat`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n});\n```\n\nTo add headers, a dynamic URL, or extra body data, see [Request Options](./request-options).\n\n## Resume a dropped stream\n\nA connection can drop in the middle of a reply. If the server sends SSE `id:` values, `fetchServerSentEvents` can continue the reply:\n\n- **Reconnect.** If the connection drops after an id arrived, the adapter reconnects with `Last-Event-ID`. It removes the replayed chunks that it already has.\n- **`joinRun(runId)`.** It sends a read-only GET with `offset=-1` and the run id. The server replays an in-flight or finished run from the start.\n\nThe ids appear only when the server passes a durability adapter to `toServerSentEventsResponse`. The ids are opaque tokens that the durability adapter owns. The chat client does not create, parse, or store them. Without ids, the adapter does one plain fetch.\n\nAdd a `GET` handler next to `POST`, so that `joinRun` works in a second tab or after a reload:\n\n- **`POST`** starts new runs. It also gets the automatic reconnects, because the adapter sends the same body again with `Last-Event-ID`.\n- **`GET`** replays a known run from the start.\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  resumeServerSentEventsResponse,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({ adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"), messages, threadId, runId });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n  });\n}\n\n// joinRun hits GET ?offset=-1&runId=... (replay only, no messages sent).\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  return resumeServerSentEventsResponse({ adapter: memoryStream(request) });\n}\n```\n\nThe `GET` handler does not call a provider. On a replay, `resumeFrom()` on the durability adapter is not null (it comes from `?offset`). So the handler replays the log. If the request has no resume offset, `resumeServerSentEventsResponse` returns a 400.\n\nOther adapters resume the same way:\n\n- `xhrServerSentEvents` uses the same `id:` lines. See [React Native](./react-native).\n- `fetchHttpStream` and `xhrHttpStream` resume over NDJSON. See [HTTP Stream](./http-stream#resume-a-dropped-stream).\n\nTo pick a durability adapter for production, see [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview).\n\nSend a message and turn off the network for a moment. When the network comes back, the reply continues with no duplicate text.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Server-Sent Events\nid: transports-sse\norder: 2\ndescription: \"Stream chat chunks over Server-Sent Events with fetchServerSentEvents and toServerSentEventsResponse, and resume a stream after the connection drops.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - sse\n  - server-sent events\n  - fetchServerSentEvents\n  - toServerSentEventsResponse\n  - resumeServerSentEventsResponse\n  - resumable sse\n  - Last-Event-ID\n---\n\nYou have a normal HTTP server and you want the chat reply to stream. Use Server-Sent Events (SSE). It is the default transport. It works in all browsers, it passes through most proxies, and it is easy to debug.\n\n## 1. Return SSE from the server\n\nWrap the `chat()` stream with `toServerSentEventsResponse()`:\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({\n    adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"),\n    messages,\n    threadId,\n    runId,\n  });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream);\n}\n```\n\n## 2. Connect from the client\n\nPass `fetchServerSentEvents()` to `useChat`:\n\n```typescript\nimport { useChat, fetchServerSentEvents } from \"@tanstack/ai-react\";\n\nconst { messages, sendMessage } = useChat({\n  connection: fetchServerSentEvents(\"/api/chat\"),\n});\n```\n\nTo add headers, a dynamic URL, or extra body data, see [Request Options](./request-options).\n\n## Resume a dropped stream\n\nA connection can drop in the middle of a reply. If the server sends SSE `id:` values, `fetchServerSentEvents` can continue the reply:\n\n- **Reconnect.** If the connection drops after an id arrived, the adapter reconnects with `Last-Event-ID`. It removes the replayed chunks that it already has.\n- **`joinRun(runId)`.** It sends a read-only GET with `offset=-1` and the run id. The server replays an in-flight or finished run from the start.\n\nThe ids appear only when the server passes a durability adapter to `toServerSentEventsResponse`. The ids are opaque tokens that the durability adapter owns. The chat client does not create, parse, or store them. Without ids, the adapter does one plain fetch.\n\nAdd a `GET` handler next to `POST`, so that `joinRun` works in a second tab or after a reload:\n\n- **`POST`** starts new runs. It also gets the automatic reconnects, because the adapter sends the same body again with `Last-Event-ID`.\n- **`GET`** replays a known run from the start.\n\n```typescript\nimport {\n  chat,\n  chatParamsFromRequest,\n  memoryStream,\n  resumeServerSentEventsResponse,\n  toServerSentEventsResponse,\n} from \"@tanstack/ai\";\nimport { openaiText } from \"@tanstack/ai-openai\";\n\nexport async function POST(request: Request) {\n  const { messages, threadId, runId } = await chatParamsFromRequest(request);\n  const stream = chat({ adapter: openaiText(\"gpt-5.6\"), messages, threadId, runId });\n  return toServerSentEventsResponse(stream, {\n    durability: { adapter: memoryStream(request) },\n  });\n}\n\n// joinRun hits GET ?offset=-1&runId=... (replay only, no messages sent).\nexport async function GET(request: Request) {\n  return resumeServerSentEventsResponse({ adapter: memoryStream(request) });\n}\n```\n\nThe `GET` handler does not call a provider. On a replay, `resumeFrom()` on the durability adapter is not null (it comes from `?offset`). So the handler replays the log. If the request has no resume offset, `resumeServerSentEventsResponse` returns a 400.\n\nOther adapters resume the same way:\n\n- `xhrServerSentEvents` uses the same `id:` lines. See [React Native](./react-native).\n- `fetchHttpStream` and `xhrHttpStream` resume over NDJSON. See [HTTP Stream](./http-stream#resume-a-dropped-stream).\n\nTo pick a durability adapter for production, see [Resumable Streams](../resumable-streams/overview).\n\nSend a message and turn off the network for a moment. When the network comes back, the reply continues with no duplicate text.\n"}
